<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Dynasty of Egypt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> ruled from about 2890 to 2686 BCE, the second line of kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>. Its rulers included Hotepsekhemwy, Nynetjer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peribsen">Peribsen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khasekhemwy">Khasekhemwy</a>, and the period seems to have seen internal division before Khasekhemwy reunited the country. It was followed by the Third Dynasty and the Old Kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-dynasty-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Second Dynasty of Egypt" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Geni · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khasekhemwy_statue_Ashmolean.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Second Dynasty is the latter of the two dynasties of the Egyptian Archaic Period, when the seat of government was centred at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thinis">Thinis</a>. It is best known for its last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khasekhemwy">Khasekhemwy</a>, and is otherwise one of the most obscure periods of Egyptian history. Archaeological evidence is very scant, but contrasting data from the First and Third Dynasties indicates important institutional and economic developments during these years.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> states that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thinis">Thinis</a> was the capital, as in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a>, but the first three kings were buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>, which suggests that the centre of power had moved to Memphis. For the first three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> the sources are fairly close in agreement, and their order is supported by an inscription on the statuette of Hetepdief, who served in the mortuary cults of these kings. The identity of the next few rulers is unclear: surviving sources may give a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> name, a Nebty name or a birth name, or may name entirely different individuals or names with no firm historical basis.</p><p>It has been theorised that after the reign of Nynetjer the country was split and ruled by two successors, because the administration of the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> had become overly complex. The annual records on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> stone survive only to the end of the reign of Nebra and for parts of that of Nynetjer, so little can be said of events.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The dynasty saw the advent of the first known complete sentences in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs">Egyptian hieroglyphs</a>. Probably the first is a seal impression from the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peribsen">Peribsen</a>, dating to about 2660 to 2650 BCE, which relates to the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>: He of Ombos has joined the Two Lands for his son, the Dual King Peribsen.</p><p>The base of a statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khasekhemwy">Khasekhemwy</a> shows slain enemies on its four sides, recording a campaign against the northern rebels, the inhabitants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>. The number of dead is given as 47,209. The scene seems to record his victory over a northern population led by its ruler, Besh, a name that may be a variant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peribsen">Peribsen</a> or that of a Libyan tribe near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Fayum</a>. The unification of Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> might have occurred under Khasekhemwy, since many Egyptologists read his name as the Two Powers arise. The king crushing enemies at his feet remained a central theme of royal iconography for three millennia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Dynasty of Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Egyptian objects inscribed with royal names from the late <b>Second Dynasty</b> onwards have been found in its temples, and Egyptian texts called large seagoing vessels ‘Byblos ships’, after the voyages that brought back timber and resin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Second Dynasty</b> saw unrest, with Peribsen adopting Seth rather than Horus as his patron, before Khasekhemwy reunited the country.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-dynastic-egypt">Early Dynastic Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
